The validator. `AssemblyValidator.validate` compared the consumer arrow's operand with the actor's
message type as TEXT — `m.text` against `messageType.namePath` — the third consumer of the type
vocabulary beside the two renderers, where the unit type caught on 2026-10-21 (DRACO.md §6). Over the
swept tree the OLD validator fails both assembly suites, run for the record: WorldAssemblyTest's
service reports four errors ("binding 'domains.aerial.Input.worldConsumer' expects a consumer of World
but 'domains.world.Consumer' receives domains.world.World", the same for Consumer.provider and
Provider.target, and "Output.consumer expects a consumer of Dracon but 'domains.terrestrial.Consumer'
receives draco.format.dracon.Dracon") and AerialAssemblyTest's chain one. Now `identity(td, text)`
resolves a name through `NameResolution.recorded(td)` — the definition's own record as the scope, the
reading the generator gives a recorded definition — and completes a bare result with the definition's
own package, the compact form's convention for a bare name; both sides pass through it, the consumer's
operand through the from-definition and the actor's message type through the to-definition, and the
two identities are compared by `namePath`: name and package, parameters free, as definitions are keyed.
Nothing is loaded or discovered. The actor's message type is a TypeName anchor the carrier holds in
full — the surface elides Provider's own-package `messageType World`, the JSON carries
`domains.world.World` — so the completion is exercised today only on the consumer side. The error
message spells both identities in full. The validator's own `fold` default took a named lambda where it
had taken Predef's `identity`, which the new method shadows.

